## Substitute n = 25 into the formula
T_n &= 45(0.9)^{n-1}
T_{25} &= 45(0.9)^{25-1}
&= 45(0.9)^{24}
## Evaluate and round
&= 3.5894...
&= 3.6
ANSWER: 3.6\ \text{cm}

## Identify the series
The total distance after 40 swings is the sum of the first 40 arc lengths: a geometric series with $a = 45$ and $r = 0.9$.
S_n &= \frac{a(1-r^n)}{1-r}
## Substitute n = 40
S_{40} &= \frac{45(1-0.9^{40})}{1-0.9}
## Evaluate and round
&= 443.3486...
&= 443
ANSWER: 443\ \text{cm}

## Write the angles as a geometric series
Each angle is 90% of the one before, so the angles are $26, 26(0.9), 26(0.9)^2, \dots$ with $a = 26$ and $r = 0.9$.
$|r| < 1$, so the series has a sum to infinity.
S_\infty &= \frac{a}{1-r}
## Substitute and evaluate
S_\infty &= \frac{26}{1-0.9}
&= \frac{26}{0.1}
&= 260
ANSWER: 260^\circ

## Link distance to angle
Each arc length is the radius times its angle, so the distance is in proportion to the angle. Half the total angle therefore gives half the total distance.
## Find the total distance
The arcs form a geometric series with $a = 45$ and $r = 0.9$.
S_\infty &= \frac{45}{1-0.9}
&= \frac{45}{0.1}
&= 450
## Halve it
\text{Distance} &= \frac{450}{2}
&= 225
ANSWER: 225\ \text{cm}

## Set T(t) = 82
75e^{-0.081t} + 20 &= 82
75e^{-0.081t} &= 62
e^{-0.081t} &= \frac{62}{75}
## Take natural logs of both sides
$\ln(e^k) = k$, so taking $\ln$ removes the exponential.
-0.081t &= \ln\left(\frac{62}{75}\right)
t &= \frac{\ln(62/75)}{-0.081}
## Evaluate and convert to seconds
t &= 2.3500...\text{ minutes}
&= 2.3500 \times 60 \text{ seconds}
&= 141 \text{ seconds}
ANSWER: 2\text{ minutes }21\text{ seconds}

## Differentiate T(t)
Chain rule on $e^{-0.081t}$: the derivative is $-0.081e^{-0.081t}$. The constant 20 differentiates to 0.
T(t) &= 75e^{-0.081t} + 20
T'(t) &= 75(-0.081)e^{-0.081t}
&= -6.075e^{-0.081t}
## Set T'(t) = -4.05 and solve
-6.075e^{-0.081t} &= -4.05
e^{-0.081t} &= \frac{4.05}{6.075}
&= \frac{2}{3}
## Find the temperature
Put $e^{-0.081t} = \frac{2}{3}$ straight into $T(t)$.
T &= 75\left(\frac{2}{3}\right) + 20
&= 50 + 20
&= 70
ANSWER: 70^\circ\text{C}

## Write down the rates
The volume is decreasing, so the rate is negative.
\frac{dV}{dt} &= -\frac{1}{20}
V &= x^3
\frac{dV}{dx} &= 3x^2
## Find the side length when V = 1/64
x^3 &= \frac{1}{64}
x &= \frac{1}{4}
## Use the chain rule
\frac{dx}{dt} &= \frac{dV}{dt} \div \frac{dV}{dx}
&= -\frac{1}{20} \div 3x^2
&= -\frac{1}{60x^2}
## Substitute x = 1/4
\frac{dx}{dt} &= -\frac{1}{60\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^2}
&= -\frac{1}{\frac{15}{4}}
&= -\frac{4}{15}
ANSWER: -\frac{4}{15}\ \text{cm/sec}

## Substitute t = 41/6 into V(t)
V\left(\frac{41}{6}\right) &= 60 + 41\left(\frac{41}{6}\right) - 3\left(\frac{41}{6}\right)^2
&= 60 + \frac{1681}{6} - \frac{1681}{12}
&= \frac{2401}{12}
&= 200.0833\ldots
&\approx 200
ANSWER: 200 \text{ litres}

## Find |AC| with the distance formula
|AC| &= \sqrt{(16-4)^2 + (11-2)^2}
&= \sqrt{12^2 + 9^2}
&= \sqrt{225}
&= 15
## Use the ratio 2 : 1
$|AD| : |DC| = 2 : 1$, so $|AD|$ is $\frac{2}{3}$ of $|AC|$.
|AD| &= \frac{2}{3}(15)
&= 10
ANSWER: |AD| = 10

## Find half the chord, |DB|
A line from the centre to the midpoint of a chord is perpendicular to the chord, so $CDB$ is a right-angled triangle with the right angle at $D$.
|DB| &= \frac{1}{2} \times 4\sqrt{3}
&= 2\sqrt{3}
## Find |CD| using the distance formula
|CD|^2 &= (3-1)^2 + (2-(-2))^2
&= 2^2 + 4^2
&= 20
|CD| &= \sqrt{20}
&= 2\sqrt{5}
## Use Pythagoras in triangle CDB
The radius is $r = |CB|$, the hypotenuse of the right-angled triangle.
r^2 &= |CD|^2 + |DB|^2
&= (2\sqrt{5})^2 + (2\sqrt{3})^2
&= 20 + 12
&= 32
## Take the square root and simplify
r &= \sqrt{32}
&= \sqrt{16 \times 2}
&= 4\sqrt{2}
ANSWER: r = 4\sqrt{2}

## Use the formula with A = θ/2
If $A = \frac{\theta}{2}$ then $2A = \theta$.
\cos\theta &= \cos^2\frac{\theta}{2} - \sin^2\frac{\theta}{2}
## Find cos(θ/2) from a right-angled triangle
$\sin\frac{\theta}{2} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}$: opposite side $1$, hypotenuse $\sqrt{5}$.
\text{adjacent}^2 &= (\sqrt{5})^2 - 1^2
&= 4
So the adjacent side is $2$. Since $0 \le \frac{\theta}{2} \le \frac{\pi}{2}$, the cosine is positive.
\cos\frac{\theta}{2} &= \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}
## Substitute into the formula
\cos\theta &= \left(\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}\right)^2 - \left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\right)^2
&= \frac{4}{5} - \frac{1}{5}
&= \frac{3}{5}
ANSWER: \cos\theta = \frac{3}{5}
